WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.340
Hey folks, happy Wednesday or Sunday or whichever
day it is you’re watching this.

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:08.380
I figure instead of spending days engineering
the perfect 15 minute video script while I

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:11.750
could be spending time with my family, I’m
just gonna spend five minutes shamelessly

00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:16.270
riffing on a concept brought up in my comment
section, that being slot compression.

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:21.470
The idea of slot compression is basically
this—you can fit more stuff into your deck,

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:25.849
more things of each category like finishers
or draw or removal, if you pick out and play

00:00:25.849 --> 00:00:28.150
cards that can do multiple things.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:32.250
For midrange decks, an easy way to do slot
compression is to run decently-statted creatures

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:34.660
that do other things your deck wants to do.

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:38.660
In a dirt cheap Rakdos deck I built a while
back, the creature count is decently high,

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:42.170
but the vast majority of the creatures in
the deck either draw me cards or make my opponents

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:43.460
discard cards.

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:47.350
Even among the more finisher-oriented cards
that don’t fit into these two categories,

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:51.649
most are blood artist effects, which perform
a secondary duty of discouraging board wipes,

00:00:51.649 --> 00:00:53.610
so the compression is still there.

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:56.860
My Glissa deck is a more high-curved example
of this principle.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:00.410
Fun fact—if you pay a lot of mana for a
creature, it will often be great for multiple

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:01.510
things.

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:06.740
My deck caches in a hefty 20 or so slots up
front for ramp, but it is rewarded for this

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:11.420
with a creature base consisting of powerful
removal and draw spells stapled to 5/5s and

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:15.360
8/8s and 10/10s, which means that the deck
ends up being able to win games without a

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:17.950
single dedicated wincon slot.

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:20.280
Another example of this is hate bears decks.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:24.659
By running stax effects that are also creatures,
these decks are able to heavily oppress their

00:01:24.659 --> 00:01:29.430
opponents’ game plans, while still having
a boardstate to block and pressure life totals.

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:32.750
Sometimes this pressure will be pivotal to
winning, such as in my Liesa deck, and other

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:37.149
times it will serve more as an alternative
wincon or as hate toward lifepay, but regardless

00:01:37.149 --> 00:01:39.490
this flexibility is very useful.

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:43.530
For more combo-oriented decks, one way to
have plenty of space for both pieces and supporting

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:46.329
cards is to pick cards that do both.

00:01:46.329 --> 00:01:50.009
If you need a sac outlet for your combo, you’ll
naturally run into the issue that some games

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:53.170
you won’t draw one and other games you’ll
draw multiple.

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:57.030
You could run only a couple and some tutors,
but you could also run a bunch of altars in

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:00.880
a deck with artifact synergies, or sac outlets
that get buffed if you want an alternative

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:06.030
beatdown wincon, or cards like Fanatical Devotion
and Greater Good, which might just be generically

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:07.420
good cards in some decks.

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:11.360
There are also combo decks looking for a lot
of certain types of cards to go off, such

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:13.210
as with a mid-power Najeela deck.

00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:17.680
There, a reasonable plan might be throwing
in low-cost warriors you like from any color,

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:21.010
which will further the beats and combo potential
offered by the commander while also letting

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:22.920
you have high quality creatures.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:27.030
The general gist here is that if you can make
cards needed for your combo also be valuable

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:31.470
in their own right in the deck, the combo
will feel much less space-gobbling.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:34.590
This logic also applies for heavily synergistic
decks.

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:38.560
If you have a deck focusing on a particular
card type such as equipments or auras, having

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:42.349
a lot of your supporting cards also fit into
those types means that your wincons will pack

00:02:42.349 --> 00:02:46.480
a ton of power, and it will also have the
added benefit that conditional tutors will

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:49.050
give you access to a good portion of your
deck.

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:52.860
The same goes for decks that focus on particular
creature types or particular attributes—if

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:56.510
you’re building a Goreclaw deck and your
deck’s draw and removal mostly consists

00:02:56.510 --> 00:03:00.569
of creatures with power 4 or greater, suddenly
you can fit a whole lot more beef into the

00:03:00.569 --> 00:03:03.239
deck while still having it feel smooth and
well-rounded.

00:03:03.239 --> 00:03:06.500
Finally, let’s talk lands.

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:11.230
MDFCs, utility lands, and other dual mode
cards that get you lands are great, and I

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:13.330
highly encourage using these.

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:18.069
I sometimes see people run land counts in
the low 30s in decks that aren’t that low-curved,

00:03:18.069 --> 00:03:22.450
and while I appreciate the desire to draw
gas, I echo Interesting_Failure’s thinking

00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:27.099
here: at least do your future self a favor
and throw in some MDFCs or utility lands to

00:03:27.099 --> 00:03:29.920
bring your count up to 35 or 36.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:34.019
This goes for high-curved decks running 36
lands as well—don’t be afraid to MDFC

00:03:34.019 --> 00:03:38.560
your way up to 38 or 39, or even… break
into the 40s, ooooh.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:44.129
I plan to do a longer video about lands and
manabases sometime soon so I’ll save further

00:03:44.129 --> 00:03:48.510
discussion of the subject until then, but
long story short—lands are good, and people

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:50.880
often don’t play enough of them in casual
EDH.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:55.680
I’ve discussed some different ways to “compact
your slots”, you know, to run cards that

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:59.730
satisfy multiple things your deck wants, and
I think a logical question to ask regarding

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:04.370
this is “exactly how valuable is it to have
compacted slots?”

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:08.100
I like to think I have laid out a decent case
for doing such a thing—you can run more

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:11.689
of each supporting type of card while still
maintaining a solid core to your deck, and

00:04:11.689 --> 00:04:15.579
you can also run a higher land count for purposes
of not getting screwed while mitigating some

00:04:15.579 --> 00:04:17.519
of the downsides of mana flood.

00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:22.540
However, an important thing to remember is
this—at its core, your deck still needs

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:25.120
to do a thing that wins.

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:29.009
In the past I’ve sometimes fallen deep into
the hole of synergy and support cards, and

00:04:29.009 --> 00:04:34.320
built a deck that runs very smoothly and feels
good at the table, but ultimately lacks substantive

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:38.270
wincons and usually just succumbs to getting
outscaled by somebody else.

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:42.449
I advocate for people to put more support
cards in their decks because I think the average

00:04:42.449 --> 00:04:48.020
EDH player is on the opposite end of the snappy-to-smooth
spectrum as me, but it’s good to keep in

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:52.259
the back of your head that playing smoothly
and having interaction isn’t a substitute

00:04:52.259 --> 00:04:55.600
for having a good way to end the game—you
should try to have both of these things in

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:55.960
your deck.

